<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kronborg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1574–1585</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Kronborg Castle</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kronborg is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> castle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingor">Helsingør</a> on the narrowest point of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">Øresund</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> collected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sound-toll">Sound Toll</a> from passing ships. Frederick II rebuilt an earlier fortress there between 1574 and 1585, and Christian IV restored it after a fire in 1629. Known as Elsinore in Shakespeare's Hamlet, it is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kronborg.jpg" alt="Kronborg" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ArildV · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kronborg_April_2026_10.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
